* Index::               Complete index.
@end menu

@node Quick Start
@chapter Quick Start

@example
import perlthon

# Evaluate Perl code directly
perlthon.eval('"Hello " . "from Perl!"')  # "Hello from Perl!"

# Load and use Perl modules
posix = perlthon.use("POSIX")
posix.floor(3.7)   # 3.0
posix.ceil(3.2)    # 4.0

# Call fully qualified Perl functions
perlthon.call("POSIX::floor", 3.7)  # 3.0
@end example

@node API Reference
@chapter API Reference

@menu
* perlthon.use::        Load a Perl module.
* perlthon.eval::       Evaluate Perl code.
* perlthon.call::       Call a Perl function.
* PerlModule::          Module proxy object.
@end menu

@node perlthon.use
@section @code{perlthon.use(module_name)}

Load a Perl module, equivalent to Perl's @code{use} statement.  Returns a
@code{PerlModule} proxy object where attribute access maps to function
calls in that module's namespace.

@example
json = perlthon.use("JSON::PP")
@end example

The module is loaded idempotently --- calling @code{use()} multiple times
for the same module is safe and will not raise an error.

Raises @code{RuntimeError} if the module cannot be found or loaded.

@node perlthon.eval
@section @code{perlthon.eval(code)}

Evaluate a string of Perl code and return the result, automatically
converted to a Python type.

@example
perlthon.eval('[1, 2, 3]')         # [1, 2, 3]
perlthon.eval('@{"a" => 1@}')      # @{"a": 1@}
perlthon.eval('undef')             # None
@end example

Raises @code{RuntimeError} if the Perl code fails to compile or run.

@node perlthon.call
@section @code{perlthon.call(function_name, *args)}

Call a Perl function by its fully qualified name.

@example
perlthon.call("POSIX::floor", 3.7)  # 3.0
@end example

Arguments are automatically converted from Python to Perl types before
being passed to the function.

@node PerlModule
@section PerlModule Proxy

The @code{PerlModule} object returned by @code{perlthon.use()} supports
attribute access for calling functions:

@example
posix = perlthon.use("POSIX")
posix.floor(3.7)  # calls POSIX::floor(3.7)
@end example

For OO-style method dispatch (passing the module name as the invocant),
use the @code{call()} method:

@example
module.call("method", arg1, arg2)
@end example

@node Type Mapping
@chapter Type Mapping

The following type conversions are performed automatically:

@multitable @columnfractions .4 .4
@headitem Python @tab Perl
@item @code{str} @tab String scalar
@item @code{int} @tab Integer scalar (IV)
@item @code{float} @tab Float scalar (NV)
@item @code{bool} @tab @code{$true} / @code{$false}
@item @code{None} @tab @code{undef}
@item @code{list} @tab Array reference
@item @code{dict} @tab Hash reference
@end multitable

@node Architecture
@chapter Architecture

Perlthon has a layered architecture:

@example
Python <-> perlthon (Python) <-> _core (Rust/PyO3) <-> perl_glue.c <-> libperl
@end example

@table @file
@item perlthon/
Pure Python API layer providing @code{use}, @code{eval}, @code{call}, and
@code{PerlModule}.

@item src/lib.rs
Rust FFI bindings to the C glue, plus PyO3 wrapper classes exposing
@code{PerlInterpreter} to Python.

@item csrc/perl_glue.c
Thin C wrapper around Perl's macro-heavy embedding API.  Handles
interpreter lifecycle, @code{eval_pv}, @code{call_pv}, @code{call_method},
SV type introspection, and value marshalling.

@item build.rs
Build script that discovers Perl via @code{ExtUtils::Embed}, compiles the
C glue, and handles @code{libperl.so} discovery.
@end table

@node Prerequisites
@chapter Prerequisites

@itemize @bullet
@item Python >= 3.12
@item Perl development headers (@code{libperl-dev} on Debian/Ubuntu)
@item @code{libcrypt-dev} (on Debian/Ubuntu)
@item Rust toolchain (for building from source)
@end itemize

